    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d231">

                <pb facs="0386" n="294" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v01/pg294"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72119/1825</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Vice Consul at Zurich</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >McNally</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d231-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Zürich</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-07-27T12:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >undated</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received July 29,
                            1918, 6.14 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>My son-in-law has been transferred to the Admiralty in Berlin and there the
                    German Chancellor Hertling requested him to ask me to transmit the President of
                    the United States through the Department of State an acceptance of his peace
                    conditions saying that in no other way could he get the true statement before
                    him. I replied that I was not permitted to without the consent of the Department
                    of State and there the matter has rested for several days. Believing now,
                    however, that my Government would profit by a knowledge of its nature, I am
                    transmitting unknown to them the complete text: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>German Chancellor fully agrees to the four conditions in President
                            Wilson’s speech at Mount Vernon on the Fourth of July, 1918, and he too
                            considers them as a good basis for peace negotiations. He agrees to
                            these four conditions in the belief that the first generally is not
                            intended to apply to any one stated government but is directed against
                            all autocracies generally.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">McNally</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
